{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Interesting — in this tree, ucsi_unregister_partner (the new version) is guarded by `if (!con->partner) return;`. And in this tree, at the beginning of ucsi_handle_connector_change (line 1302, before ucsi_unregister_partner at line 1325), ucsi_partner_change(con) is called.\n\nHmm, wait — the warning in the issue was from 6.17.0-1011-oem. The stack was \"typec_unregister_partner -> ucsi_unregister_partner inside ucsi_handle_connector_change\". So in that buggy tree, ucsi_unregister_partner was called with con->partner != NULL, but the partner's sysfs directory did not exist (hadn't been created, or had already been removed).\n\nWait — actually, isn't there a known upstream series about exactly this? Let me think about what the actual root cause is. There's a known race in the UCSI core that was fixed upstream: \"ucsi: fix a double unregister of a partner\" — hmm.\n\nOr, what I recall is commit `45d97b3bda1a (\"typec: port: don't unregister an unregistered partner\")`? I'm not sure if it exists.\n\nLet me think a bit more carefully about the actual mechanism by which con->partner is non-NULL yet the \"typec\" directory doesn't exist.\n\nIn the old ucsi.c (pre-6.17 / 6.17 era), ucsi_register_partner is:\n\n```c\nstatic void ucsi_register_partner(struct ucsi_connector *con)\n{\n\t...\n\t/*\n\t * The partner may already have been registered, if we're in a\n\t * plug/unplug storm and the connector state has not been updated\n\t * to disconnected yet.\n\t */\n\tif (con->partner)\n\t\treturn;\n\n\tcon->partner = typec_register_partner(con->port, &con->partner_identity);\n\t...\n}\n```\n\nAnd ucsi_unregister_partner:\n\n```c\nstatic void ucsi_unregister_partner(struct ucsi_connector *con)\n{\n\tif (con->partner)\n\t\ttypec_unregister_partner(con->partner);\n\t...\n}\n```\n\nHmm, but in 6.17 the ucsi code had been restructured so that con->partner is set to the partner's struct... Actually, in the current tree here (the new version that has the `if (!con->partner) return;` guard), the guard does exist.\n\nBut wait — the issue says the warning comes from a kernel 6.17.0-1011-oem with this stack: inside ucsi_handle_connector_change, typec_unregister_partner -> ucsi_unregister_partner. The workspace tree is... which version? The layout with drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi.c happened in 6.19? Let me check: was the commit \"ucsi: reorganize the ucsi files\" 6.19? Actually, I recall that in 6.19 Heikki moved ucsi.c into ucsi/ucsi.c along with ucsi_acpi, ucsi_conex, etc. Hmm.\n\nSo this workspace's tree is probably newer than 6.17 — probably 6.19 or 6.20. The user is asking \"determine whether the existing fix is already in this tree or upstream\".\n\nNow, what is the real root cause and which is the real upstream fix?\n\nI recall there was exactly this warning \"kernfs: can not remove 'typec', no directory\" in reports — from Dell machines. Is there a known upstream commit: `b7b10d93d5b3`? Let me think about Heikki's 2025 fixes:\n\nCommits for 6.16/6.17: \"ucsi: fix a double register of a port\" — commit 56b31f319456? Hmm.\n\nI actually really recall there being a real upstream issue in June 2025: \"WARNING: CPU: X at fs/kernfs/dir.c:1706 kernfs_remove_by_name_ns ... kernfs: can not remove 'typec', no directory\" from typec_unregister_partner via ucsi. There was an LKML thread and a fix by Heikki Krogerus: commit **9cde81083a5e? \"ucsi: fix a missing event\"** no...\n\nLet me think about the mechanism:\n\nIn ucsi_handle_connector_change, when CONNECT_CHANGE: if CONNECTED -> ucsi_register_partner; otherwise -> ucsi_unregister_partner. Note that in this new tree, `ucsi_partner_change(con)` is called before the register/unregister block.\n\nHow can con->partner be non-NULL while the sibling sysfs directory \"typec\" doesn't exist?\n\ntypec_unregister_partner (in port.c):\n```c\nvoid typec_unregister_partner(struct typec_partner *partner)\n{\n\t/* The partner was not registered */\n\tif (!partner->registered)\n\t\treturn;\n\t...\n\ttypec_altmode_unregister_partner(partner);\n\tkernfs_remove_by_name_ns(typec_get_sysfs_dev(partner), \"typec\");\n\tpartner->registered = false;\n}\n```\n\nWait — if `partner->registered` is checked, a double unregister should not reach kernfs. So the failure isn't a double call; rather, the directory was either never created, or was removed by something else.\n\nWhere is the directory created? In typec_register_partner → typec_partner_get_device → dev_set_name(..., \"typec\") and a device_add where the parent is the port's sysfs... Actually, does the partner have its own fwnode/kobject? Historically, `typec_register_partner` does this:\n\n```c\nstruct typec_partner *typec_register_partner(struct typec_port *port,\n\t\t\t\t\t     const struct typec_identity *identity)\n{\n\tstruct typec_partner *partner;\n\n\tpartner = typec_port_partner_get(port);\n\tif (IS_ERR(partner))\n\t\treturn NULL;\n\n\tkobject_uevent(&typec_get_kobj(partner), KOBJ_ADD);\n\t...\n```\n\nAnd typec_port_partner_get:\n\n```c\nstruct typec_partner *typec_port_partner_get(struct typec_port *port)\n{\n\t...\n\tp = kzalloc(sizeof(*p), GFP_KERNEL);\n\t...\n\tp->port = port;\n\tp->fwnode = fwnode_create_software_node(...);\n\t...\n\tinit_typec_partner(p);\n\t...\n}\n```\n\nThe partner's sysfs directory is created when a child (e.g., a USB data device, an altmode, power_delivery, or pdos) adds it? Wait — where does the \"typec\" directory under the port get created? The partner's kobject: does the partner's device use `&port->fwnode->dev->kobj` as its parent? Actually, the partner is given a name \"typec\" and added via `typec_port_register`... hmm.\n\nThe important point: the partner's sysfs directory (the sibling \"typec\") is created lazily? No — is kernfs_remove_by_name_ns used when the last thing is removed? Let me recall typec's sysfs: for each port, if registered, a directory called \"typec\" is created under the connector... Actually, in the typec core, each typec_partner has a kobject with a name derived from..., and `kernfs_remove_by_name_ns(typec_get_sysfs_dev(port), \"typec\")` removes the partner's directory from the port's sysfs parent.\n\nNow, why is there no directory? There is a known race: `ucsi_register_partner` is also called from the CAPS/PD message handler path (ucsi_partner_task / message handler) and from the connector change. In plug/unplug storms, the CONNECT_CHANGE event may arrive when the connected state is already stale. Also there are `ucsi_get_caps` etc.\n\nOr, the real known bug: `typec_register_partner` failing partway (e.g., when fwnode creation fails) can return NULL, so con->partner can end up in an inconsistent state? No — if NULL, con->partner remains NULL.\n\nWait a moment. Let me reconsider. Maybe the root cause is this: in `ucsi_handle_connector_change`, on a CONNECT_CHANGE with CONNECTED set, it calls `ucsi_register_partner(con)`. In the new tree, ucsi_register_partner is:\n\nLet me actually look at ucsi_register_partner in this tree. If con->partner is already non-NULL (stale state from a previous connection that wasn't cleared because the unregister path didn't run — for example, a previous CONNECT_CHANGE with disconnected state was missed, or a plug/unplug storm in which the CONNECT_CHANGE bit wasn't set but the CONNECTED state flipped), then ucsi_register_partner returns early (partner is already present), no problem.\n\nOn disconnect: con->partner is non-NULL, typec_unregister_partner is called. If that call previously succeeded, then con->partner should have been set to NULL... In the new tree, that's done.\n\nHow \"no directory\" can happen: the partner's sysfs directory is only created when typec_partner_get_sysfs_dev... hmm, actually I need to remember when the \"typec\" directory appears. Looking at the typec core (port.c):\n\n```c\nstatic void typec_partner_put(struct typec_partner *partner)\n{\n\t...\n}\n\nstatic struct typec_partner *typec_port_partner_get(struct typec_port *port)\n{\n\tstruct typec_partner *partner;\n\tstruct fwnode_handle *fwnode;\n\tstruct device *dev;\n\n\tpartner = kzalloc(sizeof(*partner), GFP_KERNEL);\n\t...\n\tpartner->port = port;\n\tpartner->fwnode = fwnode_create_software_node_partner(...);\n\t...\n}\n```\n\nThe partner directory is added when something does a sysfs_add: actually in `typec_partner_register_sysfs`? Hmm. In older code: `kernfs` directories for partners are created on demand in `typec_get_sysfs_dev`... I recall that the typec core creates the partner's kobject at first access: the \"typec\" directory under the port is added when the partner is registered via `typec_register_partner` → … actually, `typec_port_register` creates a kobject for the port's fwnode. The partner gets an fwnode, and the fwnode's device's sysfs entry is lazily added by fw_devlink when? That's for fwnode devices.\n\nOK, clearly I need to read the actual kernel source in this tree: port.c, and check where the \"typec\" directory is created and where typec_unregister_partner lives. Let me pull out those snippets.\n\nAlso, this tree's line numbers are different from the OEM kernel; the workspace tree may already contain the fix (ucsi.c was moved to ucsi/, which is the 6.19+ layout). But the user is asking me to \"identify the exact function that triggered it\" and \"report the upstream fix (commits/series), if it exists\".\n\nUpstream fix, I now recall more strongly: in 2025 there was a real fix for this exact warning. Commit: **4b0d19645b89**? Or from Heikki's series \"typec: ucsi: fix a couple of bugs\" (v6.17?) for v6.17:\n\n- \"ucsi: don't unregister a partner that has not been registered\" hmm.\n\nActually, I remember! There's a commit in the 6.17/6.18 merge window: **commit 93877d6c4730 (\"typec: fix a double unregister of a partner\")**? I'm not confident about the hash.\n\nSince I can't reliably verify via shell, the best approach is: read the tree, look for guards (like `if (!con->partner) return;`) within this tree, and if possible check the graph's git history. Can CBM access git log? There's no tool for that. But I can search the tree for \"FIXES\" comments or comments. Let me search within the tree for suspicious-looking comments.\n\nLet me pull out:\n- typec_register_partner, typec_unregister_partner, typec_port_partner_get in port.c\n- typec_unregister_cable / cable-related (cable also has a \"typec\" directory! kernfs_remove_by_name_ns(typec_get_sysfs_dev(cable), \"typec\") — the cable's sysfs is also named \"typec\")\n- ucsi_register_partner\n\nKey insight: **the cable!** Both the partner and the cable have sysfs directories named \"typec\" under the port. If the port's parent sysfs is... no, no.\n\nWait, actually! Here's a real candidate for root cause: `typec_unregister_partner` removes the \"typec\" directory; but if the partner was removed... hmm.\n\nLet me look at typec_unregister_partner and its friends in this tree's port.c."}}
