{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"this tree is considerably newer: `typec_register_partner(con->port, &desc)` takes `struct typec_partner_desc *` — this is 2025 refactoring (commits in Heikki's merge window 6.17/6.18? Actually \"typec: rework partner/cable registration\" introduced typec_partner_desc in 6.18? Hmm, I feel like it was commit 8b173f0550f2? Not sure). Also `device_unregister(&partner->dev)` — the new \"make typec partner/cable real devices\" refactoring (Heikki, 6.19, \"typec: make partner and cable real devices\"?). In the older code (the 6.17 era), typec_unregister_partner was:\n\n```c\nvoid typec_unregister_partner(struct typec_partner *partner)\n{\n\t/* The partner was not registered */\n\tif (!partner->registered)\n\t\treturn;\n\n\ttypec_altmode_unregister_partner(partner);\n\n\tkernfs_remove_by_name_ns(typec_get_sysfs_dev(partner), \"typec\");\n\n\tpartner->registered = false;\n\t...unregister the usb device...\n}\n```\n\nAnd typec_register_partner was:\n\n```c\nstruct typec_partner *typec_register_partner(struct typec_port *port,\n\t\tconst struct typec_identity *identity)\n{\n\t...\n\tpartner = typec_port_partner_get(port);\n\tif (IS_ERR(partner)) return NULL;\n\n\tkernfs_create_dir_ns? ...\n```\n\nActually in old code, the \"typec\" directory is created in `typec_partner_register`... hmm: in old class.c:\n\n```c\nstatic struct typec_partner *\ntypec_partner_link_usb(struct typec_partner *partner, struct device *dev)\n```\n\nand sysfs creation is: when is the partner's directory added? In old code, `typec_port_partner_get` creates a kobject... Let me recall precisely — old code:\n\n```c\nstruct typec_partner *typec_register_partner(struct typec_port *port,\n\t\tconst struct typec_identity *identity)\n{\n\t/* The partner was already registered */\n\tif (port->partner)\n\t\treturn port->partner;\n\n\tpartner = typec_port_partner_get(port);\n\tif (IS_ERR(partner))\n\t\treturn NULL;\n\n\tkobject_uevent(&typec_get_kobj(partner), KOBJ_ADD);\n\n\ttypec_partner_set_identity(partner, identity);\n\ttypec_altmode_register_partner(partner);\n\t...\n}\n```\n\nHmm, so where is the sysfs directory? `typec_get_sysfs_dev` → the partner's device `typec` under the port's device. It's created via `device_add`? In old code, the partner had a `dev`, which was... there was `device_register` on the partner? I think the old code created the partner's fwnode device via `of_software_node`/`dev_set_name(&partner->dev, NULL, \"%s\", \"typec\")`, and the directory was lazily created by sysfs when `typec_altmode_register_partner` or a link was created...\n\nThe key question for root cause: why ENOENT. In the old (6.17) ucsi code:\n\nAt 6.17 ucsi.c's `ucsi_handle_connector_change`:\n\n```c\n\tif (change & UCSI_CONSTAT_CONNECT_CHANGE) {\n\t\t...\n\t\tif (UCSI_CONSTAT(con, CONNECTED)) {\n\t\t\tret = ucsi_register_partner(con);\n\t\t\t...\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tucsi_unregister_partner(con);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n```\n\nand\n\n```c\nstatic void ucsi_unregister_partner(struct ucsi_connector *con)\n{\n\tif (con->partner)\n\t\ttypec_unregister_partner(con->partner);\n\n\tucsi_unregister_cable(con);\n\t...\n}\n```\n\nHmm, wait, in 6.17 did ucsi use a separate con->partner_identity and register the partner? 6.17's ucsi was already using `typec_register_partner(con->port, &con->partner_identity)`.\n\nNow, a race reported on Dell 5 16 with BIOS 0.4.53 — the 6.17.0-1011-oem kernel: the OEM kernel is Ubuntu's OEM kernel based on 6.17. The warning \"kernfs: can not remove 'typec', no directory\" was reported as a bug in Ubuntu (LP bug). I actually feel like I remember reading this: on some laptops it happens during resume/plug-unplug at boot.\n\nRoot cause candidates in the old code: `ucsi_register_partner` has a race where `con->partner` is set but `typec_register_partner` failed partway? In 6.17's ucsi_register_partner:\n\n```c\nstatic void ucsi_register_partner(struct ucsi_connector *con)\n{\n\t...\n\t/*\n\t * The partner may already have been registered, in this case we can\n\t * just update the identity.\n\t */\n\tif (con->partner)\n\t\tgoto update_identity;\n\t...\n\tcon->partner = typec_register_partner(con->port, &con->partner_identity);\n\t...\n```\n\nHmm, I don't remember exactly.\n\nOr, the root cause might be on the port.c side: `typec_unregister_partner` calls `kernfs_remove_by_name_ns(typec_get_sysfs_dev(partner), \"typec\")` — and if the partner's sysfs child (power_supply? no, that's the psy)... In the old code, was the partner's \"typec\" directory created only when registering a partner in `typec_register_partner`? No wait — now I remember precisely! Old code:\n\n```c\nstatic void typec_partner_register_partner_sysfs? \n```\n\nNo... old class.c has:\n\n```c\nstruct typec_partner *\ntypec_register_partner(struct typec_port *port,\n\t\t\tconst struct typec_identity *identity)\n{\n\t/* The partner was already registered */\n\tif (port->partner)\n\t\treturn port->partner;\n\n\tkobject_uevent(&port->kobj, KOBJ_CHANGE);\n\n\tpartner = device_create? \n```\n\nHmm, honestly. I should read what the *old* code in the OEM kernel looked like, but I only have this tree at hand. But the question is: \"identify the exact problematic function in drivers/usb/typec (and drivers/usb/typec/ucsi), and explain why kernfs_remove_by_name_ns fails with ENOENT\".\n\nIn this new tree, typec_unregister_partner does device_unregister — double device_unregister would result in a different warning. In the new tree the path is fundamentally different. Therefore, this workspace tree is a newer upstream (6.19/6.20 or so) than the buggy OEM kernel (6.17), and the user wants to know whether the fix landed.\n\nI need to identify the actual upstream fix. Let me dig through memory: 2025, LKML, \"kernfs: can not remove 'typec', no directory\", Dell/XPS. I think there was a commit by Heikki Krogerus (canon/Intel) for v6.17-rc or v6.18:\n\n**commit 5a7c4e91425a? \"ucsi: fix a double-unregister of a port\"** — hmm.\n\nOr I remember a series: \"typec: ucsi: two fixes\" → commit d240205a2d28 (\"ucsi: fix handling of CONNECT_CHANGE\")? Not sure.\n\nLet me try to find the actual bug in the 6.17 code from memory. 6.17's drivers/usb/typec/ucsi.c, ucsi_handle_connector_change (I'm fairly confident of this, from ~2025):\n\n```c\nstatic void ucsi_handle_connector_change(struct work_struct *work)\n{\n\tstruct ucsi_connector_work *cw = ...;\n\t...\n\tif (change & UCSI_CONSTAT_CONNECT_CHANGE) {\n\t\ttypec_set_pwr_role(con->port, role);\n\t\tucsi_port_psy_changed(con);\n\t\tucsi_partner_change(con);\n\t\tucsi_orientation(con);\n\n\t\tif (UCSI_CONSTAT(con, CONNECTED)) {\n\t\t\tret = ucsi_register_partner(con);\n\t\t\t...\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tucsi_unregister_partner(con);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n```\n\nAnd 6.17's ucsi_unregister_partner:\n\n```c\nstatic void ucsi_unregister_partner(struct ucsi_connector *con)\n{\n\tif (con->partner)\n\t\ttypec_unregister_partner(con->partner);\n\n\tucsi_unregister_cable(con);\n\tif (con->ucsi->ops->remove_partner_altmodes)\n\t\tcon->ucsi->ops->remove_partner_altmodes(con);\n\n\ttypec_partner_set_usb_power_delivery(NULL);?? \n```\n\nHmm.\n\nAnd typec_unregister_partner in 6.17's class.c:\n\n```c\nvoid typec_unregister_partner(struct typec_partner *partner)\n{\n\t/* The partner was not registered */\n\tif (!partner->registered)\n\t\treturn;\n\n\ttypec_altmode_unregister_partner(partner);\n\n\tkernfs_remove_by_name_ns(typec_get_sysfs_dev(partner), \"typec\");\n\n\tpartner->registered = false;\n\n\ttypec_partner_unlink_usb(partner);  /* if there was an usb device */\n\t...\n}\n```\n\nSo for the warning to fire, partner->registered == true but the sibling \"typec\" kobject doesn't exist. When was it created? In the old code — I just remembered!: in `typec_register_partner` → no, the sysfs directory is created in `typec_port_partner_get`?? Let me stop guessing; the old code is:\n\n```c\nstatic struct typec_partner *\ntypec_port_partner_get(struct typec_port *port)\n{\n\t...\n\tpartner->fwnode = dev_fwnode(port_fwnode(parent))? no...\n```\n\nAlright, different approach: maybe the ENOENT comes from the port's directory, not the partner's directory. `kernfs_remove_by_name_ns(kn, \"typec\")` — kn is the partner's sysfs parent device's kn (typec_get_sysfs_dev(partner) returns... for the partner: `typec_get_sysfs_dev(&partner->dev)`. For the partner, its \"sysfs device\" is the partner's own device, and the removal is done against the *port's* kn?\n\nOld code definition:\n\n```c\nstatic struct device *\ntypec_get_sysfs_dev(struct typec_partner *partner)\n{\n\treturn partner ? partner->port->dev : NULL;\n}\n```\n\nRight! I'm fairly confident: `kernfs_remove_by_name_ns(typec_get_sysfs_dev(partner), \"typec\")` removes the \"typec\" directory under the PORT device (the partner's directory lives under the port). The partner directory is created in `typec_register_partner`:\n\n```c\n\t...\n\tkernfs? \n```\n\nHmm, the directory creation in old code is:\n\n```c\nstruct typec_partner *\ntypec_register_partner(struct typec_port *port,\n\t\t\tconst struct typec_identity *identity)\n{\n\t...\n\tp = devm? no.\n\n\tp = kzalloc(...);\n\tp->port = port;\n\tp->fwnode = fwnode_create_software_node_partner(NULL, NULL);\n\t...\n\t/* Add the partner to sysfs */\n\t...\n}\n```\n\nGrr. OK. Since I can't read the OEM 6.17 tree, I'll read this tree's class.c and explain both: this tree's current implementation (device-based, 6.19+) and where the old kernfs path was. The user is explicitly asking to explain why kernfs_remove_by_name_ns fails with ENOENT in a 6.17-based OEM kernel, and to report an upstream fix if one exists.\n\nNow — what's the actual known upstream fix? Let me seriously think. There definitely was a bug on the UCSI disconnect path for exactly this warning. I remember the thread: \"UCSI: WARNING: CPU: X at fs/kernfs/dir.c:1655 kernfs_remove_by_name_ns\" reported multiple times on Dell XPS and ThinkPads. And the fix 