{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Key finding: the WARN in this tree fires when `parent == NULL` — the WARN message is \"kernfs: can not remove '%s', no directory\". In 6.17 (fs/kernfs/dir.c:1706), the same WARN fired in the `!parent` case. That means in the OEM kernel, `typec_get_sysfs_dev(partner)` returned NULL, i.e., `partner` is... wait, in the old code:\n\n```c\nstatic struct device *\ntypec_get_sysfs_dev(struct typec_partner *partner)\n{\n\treturn partner ? partner->port->dev : NULL;\n}\n```\n\nHmm, if the parent is partner->port->dev, then NULL only occurs if partner is NULL — but that's guarded. However... in the old 6.17 ucsi code, what is being passed? In 6.17: `typec_unregister_partner(con->partner)`. And the 6.17 class.c typec_unregister_partner:\n\n```c\nvoid typec_unregister_partner(struct typec_partner *partner)\n{\n\t/* The partner was not registered */\n\tif (!partner->registered)\n\t\treturn;\n\n\ttypec_altmode_unregister_partner(partner);\n\n\tkernfs_remove_by_name_ns(typec_get_sysfs_dev(partner), \"typec\");\n\t...\n```\n\nHmm, wait — actually maybe I'm confusing it with cable: typec_unregister_cable also runs kernfs_remove_by_name_ns(typec_get_sysfs_dev(cable), \"typec\")? Hmm, no, that's not it.\n\nHold on — I actually just recall the old code pretty precisely now! Old class.c, 6.17:\n\n```c\nstatic struct device *\ntypec_get_sysfs_dev(struct typec_partner *partner)\n{\n\t/* The partner is a child of the port on the sysfs */\n\treturn partner ? partner->port->dev : NULL;\n}\n```\n\nHmm, if so, parent is always non-NULL (port->dev exists). In that case the WARN fires in a different branch: `if (!parent)`. In 6.17's dir.c the code is:\n\n```c\nint kernfs_remove_by_name_ns(struct kernfs_node *parent, const char *name,\n\t\t\t     const struct ns_common *ns)\n{\n\tstruct kernfs_node *kn;\n\tstruct kernfs_root *root;\n\n\tif (!parent) {\n\t\tWARN(1, KERN_WARNING \"kernfs: can not remove '%s', no directory\\n\",\n\t\t     name);\n\t\treturn -ENOENT;\n\t}\n\t...\n```\n\nYeah, WARN only fires when !parent. So in the OEM kernel, `typec_get_sysfs_dev(partner)` returned NULL ⇒ **partner == NULL**, and typec_unregister_partner was called with NULL? But the old class.c typec_unregister_partner dereferences `partner->registered` first → if it were NULL, it should segfault, not WARN.\n\nWait. Unless the ucsi 6.17 code is passing something else. Hmm.\n\nWait a moment. If in 6.17 class.c it's `kernfs_remove_by_name_ns(typec_get_sysfs_dev(partner), \"typec\")` and typec_get_sysfs_dev returns partner->port->dev, and the **port device has already been unregistered/deregistered** (during suspend/resume, or during concurrent driver unbind), then... then the parent kernfs node is... even if the port's sysfs has been removed, typec_get_sysfs_dev doesn't return NULL, it just returns a stale pointer to the struct device whose kn has already been removed. kernfs_remove_by_name_ns on the removed kn: kernfs_root(parent) works, kernfs_find_ns fails due to the state... and it returns -ENOENT without WARN (since parent != NULL). So this doesn't match either.\n\nThe WARN message with WARN(1) only fires when parent == NULL. So the old ucsi/class code is passing a NULL parent via some path where the `registered` check passes. Therefore `typec_get_sysfs_dev(partner)` must have returned NULL, i.e., partner == NULL and partner->registered should deref... contradiction. So my memory of the old code's shape must be wrong.\n\nAlternative: the caller passing name \"typec\" and a NULL parent isn't typec_unregister_partner in the first place — maybe it's the CABLE. Old class.c:\n\n```c\nvoid typec_unregister_cable(struct typec_cable *cable)\n{\n\t/* The cable was not registered */\n\tif (!cable->registered)\n\t\treturn;\n\n\tkernfs_remove_by_name_ns(typec_get_sysfs_dev(&cable->partner), \"typec\");\n\t...\n}\n```\n\nHmm, no. But the stated stack: WARN raised from typec_unregister_partner. The user's stack: \"warning at fs/kernfs/dir.c:1706 kernfs_remove_by_name_ns — raised from typec_unregister_partner -> ucsi_unregister_partner inside ucsi_handle_connector_change\".\n\nSo within this tree, let me think about what `typec_get_sysfs_dev(partner)` looked like in 6.17. Actually, in the older typec (pre-6.x, e.g., 5.x) it might be:\n\n```c\nstatic struct device *\ntypec_get_sysfs_dev(struct typec_partner *partner)\n{\n\treturn partner ? &partner->dev : NULL;   // ???\n}\n```\n\nWait — actually, now I remember the real old code! Old typec class: the partner doesn't have its own struct device; it has a fwnode, and sysfs uses a kobject attached to the PORT:\n\n```c\n/*\n * The partner's identity etc are under the port's directory in sysfs\n * under a child directory called \"typec\"\n */\nstatic void typec_partner_register... \n```\n\nHmm, I recall `kobject_to_dev(typec_get_kobj(partner))`...\n\nHonestly, what matters for the answer is: WARN at dir.c:1706 (6.17) — in 6.17's dir.c:\n\n```c\nint kernfs_remove_by_name_ns(struct kernfs_node *parent, const char *name,\n\t\t\t     const struct ns_common *ns)\n{\n\tstruct kernfs_node *kn;\n\tstruct kernfs_root *root;\n\n\tif (!parent) {\n\t\tWARN(1, KERN_WARNING\n\t\t     \"kernfs: can not remove '%s', no directory\\n\", name);\n\t\treturn -ENOENT;\n\t}\n```\n\nLet me confirm the line numbers: is the WARN at line 1706 in 6.17? In this tree the WARN is around line 1730 (function starts at 1724). 6.17 has a slightly different layout; the dir.c:1706 in the warning matches the `!parent` WARN (roughly 24 lines before). Consistent.\n\nSo parent == NULL. In 6.17 class.c:\n\nNow I'm fairly confident in the old code (from the 5.10–6.16 era, which is 6.17):\n\n```c\nstatic void\ntypec_partner_unlink...\n\nvoid typec_unregister_partner(struct typec_partner *partner)\n{\n\t/* The partner was not registered */\n\tif (!partner->registered)\n\t\treturn;\n\n\ttypec_altmode_unregister_partner(partner);\n\n\tkernfs_remove_by_name_ns(typec_get_sysfs_dev(partner), \"typec\");\n\n\tpartner->registered = false;\n\t...\n}\n```\n\nAnd\n\n```c\nstatic struct device *\ntypec_get_sysfs_dev(struct typec_partner *partner)\n{\n\treturn partner ? partner->port->dev : NULL;\n}\n```\n\nIf this is correct, then parent should never be NULL (port->dev is valid). Contradiction. So the old typec_get_sysfs_dev must have been:\n\n```c\nstatic struct device *\ntypec_get_sysfs_dev(struct typec_partner *partner)\n{\n\t/*\n\t * The partner's sysfs directory is created when the partner is\n\t * registered, and removed when it's unregistered. The directory\n\t * lives under the port's sysfs directory.\n\t */\n\treturn partner->port->dev;\n}\n```\n\nSame thing. Unless the ucsi code is doing something different... wait, wait. Maybe in 6.17 ucsi.c, `con->partner` is not a typec_partner? In the older ucsi (5.x–6.16):\n\n```c\nstatic void ucsi_unregister_partner(struct ucsi_connector *con)\n{\n\tif (con->partner)\n\t\ttypec_unregister_partner(con->partner);\n\t...\n}\n```\n\ncon->partner is struct typec_partner*. OK.\n\nHmm, so given parent cannot be NULL... the WARN can't come from the !parent branch... unless the port device has been unregistered, meaning `partner->port->dev.kobj...` no wait, the function returns struct device* — if you pass a valid pointer, it will never be NULL.\n\nBut — the device might be in the middle of deletion: even during device_del, `partner->port->dev` is still a valid pointer. So no.\n\nHmm, so maybe in 6.17:\n\n```c\nvoid typec_unregister_partner(struct typec_partner *partner)\n{\n\t/* The partner was not registered */\n\tif (!partner)\n\t\treturn;\n\t...\n```\n\nAnd the call is passing `typec_get_sysfs_dev(partner, \"typec\")`? I keep going back and forth.\n\nAnother possibility: the WARN isn't `!parent` in 6.17 — maybe 6.17 has an additional WARN: `if (!kn) WARN(...)`. Let me check the git history of fs/kernfs/dir.c: at some point the \"no directory\" WARN was there, and... in old kernels (long ago, < 4.x):\n\n```c\n\tint kernfs_remove_by_name_ns(...)\n\t{\n\t\t...\n\t\tkn = kernfs_find(...);\n\t\tif (!kn) {\n\t\t\tWARN(1, \"kernfs: can not remove '%s', no directory\\n\", name);\n\t\t\treturn -ENOENT;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n```\n\nHmm! Actually yes — in older kernels it was WARN on !kn (child not found). Did it change at some point to WARN only on !parent? Let me check history: commit \"kernfs: remove the 'WARN' in kernfs_remove_by_name_ns()\"? There was a change by... in this tree WARN is only on !parent, and !kn silently returns -ENOENT.\n\nWhen was the !kn WARN removed? Hmm. Actually I recall: the message \"kernfs: can not remove '%s', no directory\" has been around with a WARN for a long time. Let me think about v6.17's dir.c — I actually think in 6.17 it looks like:\n\n```c\nint kernfs_remove_by_name_ns(struct kernfs_node *parent, const char *name,\n\t\t\t     const struct ns_common *ns)\n{\n\tstruct kernfs_node *kn;\n\tstruct kernfs_root *root;\n\n\tif (!parent) {\n\t\tWARN(1, KERN_WARNING \"kernfs: can not remove '%s', no directory\\n\",\n\t\t     name);\n\t\treturn -ENOENT;\n\t}\n```\n\nI think this has been the current form since the 5.x era (commit 80baedbe4f62? by Greg KH, \"kernfs: make parent NULL WARN\" hmm). Actually, yes — Greg KH's 2017 commit d6b95c64c05340548b8e73a7546a45d50b279b19 (\"sysfs: add an attribute group for the \"no\" ...\") no. Greg had a series \"kernfs fixups\" (2023–2024?) that moved the warn. Not sure.\n\nGiven dir.c:1706 in 6.17 — and in this tree (newer) the function starts at line 1724 and the WARN is around line 1729. If 6.17's dir.c is slightly shorter (before later additions, e.g. kernfs_move? etc.), the WARN around line 1706 matches the `!parent` WARN perfectly (28 lines shorter). In other words, in 6.17 the WARN is in the `!parent` branch, and 6.17's code shape = the current shape. Good — so parent == NULL.\n\nTherefore in 6.17 class.c, `typec_unregister_partner` must have been passing parent NULL ⇒ `typec_get_sysfs_dev(partner)` returns NULL ⇒ its 6.17 implementation probably was:\n\n```c\nstatic struct device *\ntypec_get_sysfs_dev(struct typec_partner *partner)\n{\n\treturn typec_partner_get_sysfs(partner); ?\n}\n```\n\nHmm. Or was the 6.17 class.c code like this:\n\n```c\n\tkernfs_remove_by_name_ns(partner->port->kobj? ...\n```\n\nHmm, hmm. Let me look at it from the ucsi side, at 6.17's ucsi.c. Actually, wait — maybe I should reconsider. Let me look at what con->partner actually is in 6.17's ucsi code. 6.17 ucsi.c (which I've seen many times):\n\n```c\nstruct ucsi_connector {\n\t...\n\tstruct typec_partner *partner;\n\tstruct typec_cable *cable;\n\t...\n};\n\nstatic void ucsi_unregister_partner(struct ucsi_connector *con)\n{\n\t/*\n\t * The partner may have been unregistered through ucsi_altmode_change,\n\t * in that case, this is a no-op.\n\t */\n\tif (!con->partner)\n\t\treturn;\n\n\ttypec_set_pwr_role(con->port, UCSI_CONSTAT(con, PWR_DIR) == UCSI_PSNK_SRC ? TYPEC_SOURCE : TYPEC_SINK);?? \n```\n\nNo, that's not it. I give up trying to recall the exact 6.17 ucsi body, but the current tree's ucsi_unregister_partner I've read above probably reflects 6.17 fairly closely (the ucsi code has been stable):\n\n```c\nstatic void ucsi_unregister_partner(struct ucsi_connector *con)\n{\n\tif (!con->partner)\n\t\treturn;\n\n\ttypec_set_mode(con->port, TYPEC_STATE_SAFE);\n\tif (con->ucsi->ops->remove_partner_altmodes)\n\t\tcon->ucsi->ops->remove_partner_altmodes(con);\n\n\ttypec_partner_set_usb_power_delivery(con->partner, NULL);\n\tucsi_unregister_partner_pdos(con);\n\tucsi_unregister_altmodes(con, UCSI_RECIPIENT_SOP);\n\tucsi_unregister_cable(con);\n\ttypec_unregister_partner(con->partner);\n\tmemset(&con->partner_identity, 0, sizeof(con->partner_identity));\n\tcon->partner = NULL;\n}\n```\n\nGood. So the guard exists. In that case, the ENOENT with parent==NULL... in this tree, typec_unregister_partner → device_unregister, no kernfs. In 6.17, typec_unregister_partner → kernfs path.\n\nOK, let me nail down 6.17's class.c typec_unregister_partner and typec_get_sysfs_dev precisely. I have a decent memory of this file (it's stable). v6.17 drivers/usb/typec/class.c:\n\n```c\nstatic ssize_t\ntypec_port_show_cc(int cc, char *buf);\n```\n\nPartner section:\n\n```c\n/*\n * USB data\n */\nvoid typec_port_link_usb2(struct typec_port *port, struct device *dev)\n{\n\tif (!is_port_registered(port))\n\t\treturn;\n\n\tport->usb2_dev = dev;\n\n\tif (port->partner)\n\t\ttypec_partner_link_usb(port->partner, port->usb2_dev);\n\t...\n}\n```\n\nAnd the partner:\n\n```c\nstatic void typec_partner_link_usbs...\n```\n\nHmm. And:\n\n```c\nstruct typec_partner *\ntypec_register_partner(struct typec_port *port,\n\t\t\tconst struct typec_identity *identity)\n{\n\t/* The partner was already registered */\n\tif (port->partner)\n\t\treturn port->partner;\n\n\tkobject_uevent(&typec_get_kobj(port), KOBJ_CHANGE);\n\n\tpartner = typec_port_partner_get(port);\n\tif (IS_ERR(partner))\n\t\treturn NULL;\n\n\tpartner->registered = true;\n\t...\n\ttypec_partner_set_identity(partner, identity);\n\ttypec_altmode_register_partner(partner);\n\n\treturn partner;\n}\n```\n\nHmm, and the partner's sysfs directory creation... wait. I now remember!!! The partner's \"typec\" directory is in `typec_partner_set_identity`?? No...\n\nIt's in `typec_partner_link_usb`:\n\n```c\n/*\n * Type-C partner USB device link. There must be an already existing\n * USB device to link the partner to...\n */\nstatic void typec_partner_link_usb(struct typec_partner *partner, struct device *usb)\n{\n\tstruct typec_port *port;\n\tstruct fwnode_handle *fwnode;\n\n\tif (!partner->registered)\n\t\treturn;\n\n\tport = partner->port;\n\t...\n\tdevice_link_add(usb, typec_port_dev(port), ...);\n}\n```\n\nNo, I can't recall.\n\nNew strategy: maybe the old code is still in the current tree, either in a header or as a compatibility shim? The new code renamed things. The old `typec_partner_get_sysfs` may be gone. Let me grep