k Greg had a commit that turned this into a dev_dbg or nothing (since callers use it as a \"try to remove\" idiom). Let me search current tree history… I can't. But the current tree shows there's now no warn on !kn, and the !parent warn has the message \"can not remove '%s', no directory\" — hmm, interesting: in the current tree the !parent branch uses the \"can not remove\" message. That's suspicious — did Greg change the !parent branch to use that message? Plausible.\n\nAnyway: in 6.17, \"can not remove 'typec', no directory\" was emitted in the !kn case: parent = the port's kn (exists), child \"typec\" not found → -ENOENT. This fully explains it and is consistent with the offset 0xe9/0xf0 (warn near the tail).\n\nSo the root cause in 6.17: `typec_unregister_partner()` calls `kernfs_remove_by_name_ns(typec_get_sysfs_dev(partner), \"typec\")`, but the port's sysfs directory \"typec\" child doesn't exist. Why doesn't it exist? Because the old class creates the \"typec\" directory for the partner lazily, or under certain conditions — or a prior teardown already removed it.\n\nLet me recall the old class more definitively one more time. I'm now remembering the old code with higher confidence. Old (5.x–6.17) class.c:\n\n```c\nstatic ssize_t\n...\n\n/*\n * The partner's sysfs directory is created when the partner is registered...\n */\n```\n\nSpecifically I remember the old ucsi… no, this is from the old **tcpm**: tcpm_typec_disconnected → typec_unregister_partner. And typec_unregister_partner in old class.c:\n\n```c\nvoid typec_unregister_partner(struct typec_partner *partner)\n{\n\t/* The partner was not registered */\n\tif (!partner->registered)\n\t\treturn;\n\n\ttypec_altmode_unregister_partner(partner);\n\ttypec_partner_set_usb_power_delivery(partner, NULL)?? no…\n\n\tkernfs_remove_by_name_ns(typec_get_sysfs_dev(partner), \"typec\");\n\n\tpartner->registered = false;\n\tpartner->port->partner = NULL;  (hmm)\n\t...\n}\n```\n\nAnd typec_register_partner:\n\n```c\nstruct typec_partner *typec_register_partner(struct typec_port *port,\n\t\tconst struct typec_identity *identity)\n{\n\t/* The partner was already registered */\n\tif (port->partner)\n\t\treturn port->partner;\n\n\tpartner = typec_port_partner_get(port);\n\tif (IS_ERR(partner))\n\t\treturn NULL;\n\n\tpartner->registered = true;   ← ? inside partner_get?\n\t...\n}\n```\n\nAnd the directory: I believe I'm now certain of the old line:\n\n```c\nstatic void typec_partner_register ... \n```\n\nNo, no more. The directory is… hmm, maybe in `typec_altmode_register_partner`? No.\n\nHonestly, whether the directory is created at partner_register (immediate) or lazily (when the first child is added) — either way, the ENOENT in this WARN means that at the time of the unregister, no \"typec\" directory existed under the port. Two candidate states:\n\n(i) The directory was removed earlier — i.e., typec_unregister_partner effectively ran twice, or typec_port_unregister removed the tree, while ucsi still holds con->partner. The old class guards with partner->registered, so a second typec_unregister_partner is a no-op… but ucsi's con->partner is set per-connector and cleared in ucsi_unregister_partner. Is there a path where the kernfs directory is removed but con->partner is not cleared and partner->registered remains true?\n\nHmm: in old ucsi, `ucsi_partner_change` — partner identity: on PARTNER_CHANGE event, ucsi calls `ucsi_get_partner_identity` and… `typec_partner_set_identity`… does that reset the directory? typec_partner_set_usb_power_delivery(partner, NULL) in old code:\n\n```c\nvoid typec_partner_set_usb_power_delivery(struct typec_partner *partner,\n\t\tconst struct typec_capability_pd *pd)\n{\n\tstruct typec_partner *new_partner = NULL;\n\t...\n\tif (!pd) {\n\t\t/* Remove the child if it was created */\n\t\t...\n\t}\n}\n```\n\nThat removes the \"power_supply\" child, not the \"typec\" directory.\n\n(ii) The directory was never created, because creation of the \"typec\" directory in the old code is conditional. And now I actually remember — the old code creates the \"typec\" directory in **`typec_partner_register_sysfs`**, which is called from `typec_port_register` when the port's partner is set up, or on the first partner registration…\n\nLet me try one last time to recall the old class.c text. I've read this file. In particular, I remember a function like:\n\n```c\n/*\n * The partner's sysfs files are created and destroyed when the partner\n * is registered and unregistered.\n */\n```\n\nAnd:\n\n```c\nstatic void\ntypec_partner_register(struct typec_partner *partner)\n{\n\t...\n}\n```\n\nHmm, hmm. No. Fine.\n\nI'll go with a mechanism-based explanation, cite what I can prove from this tree (the new code), describe the old code's behavior qualitatively, and honestly flag what's the 6.17 source vs. this tree's text.\n\nRoot cause (final version):\n- The old Type-C class modeled the partner as a bare kernfs \"typec\" directory under the port's sysfs, with its own \"registered\" bookkeeping decoupled from the actual kernfs directory's presence. `typec_unregister_partner()` (drivers/usb/typec/class.c, 6.17) unconditionally does `kernfs_remove_by_name_ns(typec_get_sysfs_dev(partner), \"typec\")` whenever `partner->registered == true`.\n- The ucsi driver (drivers/usb/typec/ucsi.c, 6.17) calls typec_unregister_partner() from ucsi_unregister_partner() for every CONNECT_CHANGE where CONNECTED==0, and keeps con->partner until that call.\n- On the Dell platform, UCSI firmware can deliver an event pair where the controller reports CONNECTED with CONNECT_CHANGE (con->partner = typec_register_partner(), registered=true), but the partner's \"typec\" sysfs directory was already removed — because the previous disconnect had already removed the directory — while ucsi's con->partner/registered bookkeeping still says \"in use\". In that stale state, `kernfs_remove_by_name_ns(port_kn, \"typec\")` can't find the child → returns -ENOENT → dir.c:1706 WARN (\"can not remove 'typec', no directory\").\n\nHmm, but wait. If in the previous disconnect ucsi_unregister_partner had run fully, con->partner=0 → this time it's a no-op. The contradiction only exists if the \"directory\" was removed without ucsi updating state, or the state exists without the directory…\n\nLet me reconsider — perhaps the directory is created lazily in the old code: I'm now recalling something specific! In the old class.c:\n\n```c\nstatic struct kobject *\ntypec_partner_create_sysfs(struct typec_partner *partner)?? \n```\n\nNo. But I do remember the comment:\n\n```c\n/*\n * The partner's sysfs directory is created lazily, when the first\n * sysfs entry is created. This is because of the way that the USB link\n * is created...\n */\n```\n\nHmm…\n\nYou know what — there's another possibility worth taking seriously that I haven't looked at at all: the port's dev.parent. In the old code `typec_get_sysfs_dev` may return `partner->port->dev` and the \"typec\" kernfs directory under it is created in `typec_port_register`… and removed in **`typec_port_unregister`** — and on this Dell, some kind of unbind/rebind of the UCSI controller's *controller device* at boot (ucsi_acpi / platform device re-probe, or USB xhci reset during boot) can cause typec_port_unregister → removal of the entire sysfs tree (including partner \"typec\" directory), while the UCSI core's workqueue still has a pending ucsi_handle_connector_change work → ucsi_unregister_partner → con->partner still non-NULL (con struct is still valid), partner->registered still true, but the kernfs children were destroyed with the port → remove_by_name can't find \"typec\" → WARN. This is a textbook race and the classic explanation for \"warning at boot, kworker, events\".\n\nI think the upstream rework (this tree) is a fix: make the port the actual device lifecycle, partner is a real device with parent = port's device, unreg goes through the refcounted device_unregister; plus the `if (!con->partner) return` in ucsi and the error handling in typec_register_partner returning ERR_PTR to ucsi.\n\nWait — I should double-check whether this exact bug has a specific pinpoint upstream commit (rather than the big rework). The big rework I'm seeing here (port/partner are real devices, bus.c, \"%s-partner\" name) — I think that's Heikki's 6.19-merge series \"typec: make the Type-C port, partner and cable into real devices\". Actually now I recall the series name better: \"typec: fix sysfs handling for ports, partners and cables\"?? And a specific commit in it, e.g.:\n\n- `typec: port: make the port a real device` \n- `typec: make partners be real devices` — with commit message explaining \"this fixes the WARN in kernfs_remove_by_name_ns()\"?\n\nAlso, before that, in 6.18/6.17-rc1, there may have been a more targeted fix: **commit 2f8c929202ba (\"ucsi: don't unregister the partner twice\")**?? I can't verify the hash.\n\nGiven the tool constraints, what I'll honestly report is: the targeted fix path is the rework (visible in this 7.0 tree), name its key changes with file:line evidence from this tree, and note that I can't cite legacy line numbers byte-for-byte because the tree has moved on… no wait, the user wants the \"exact offending functions inside drivers/usb/typec (and drivers/usb/typec/ucsi)\" — I can name them (ucsi_unregister_partner, ucsi_handle_connector_change, typec_unregister_partner) with a note that they're the same names in 6.17, and show the location in this tree.\n\nWait a moment — actually let me reconsider: the rework in this tree might not be the fix at all; the fix might be a small ucsi commit that's also in this tree. Compare the current ucsi_unregister_partner (line 1138) against what it was around 6.17. In 6.17, ucsi_unregister_partner (which I'm fairly sure of now):\n\n```c\nstatic void ucsi_unregister_partner(struct ucsi_connector *con)\n{\n\tif (!con->partner)\n\t\treturn;\n\n\ttypec_set_mode(con->port, TYPEC_STATE_SAFE);\n\tif (con->ucsi->ops->remove_partner_altmodes)\n\t\tcon->ucsi->ops->remove_partner_altmodes(con);\n\n\ttypec_partner_set_usb_power_delivery(con->partner, NULL);\n\tucsi_unregister_partner_pdos(con);\n\tucsi_unregister_altmodes(con, UCSI_RECIPIENT_SOP);\n\tucsi_unregister_cable(con);\n\ttypec_unregister_partner(con->partner);\n\tmemset(&con->partner_identity, 0, sizeof(con->partner_identity));\n\tcon->partner = NULL;\n}\n```\n\nThis is identical to the current tree (I read above). So ucsi was essentially untouched between 6.17 and 7.0 in this function. So the fix is the rework of the typec class.\n\nAlso, in this tree, ucsi_register_partner can fail (con->partner remains NULL on error; note that ucsi_handle_connector_change currently ignores the return? — line 1313 `ucsi_register_partner(con);` hmm, actually the current code calls `ucsi_register_partner(con);` without checking the return?? Looking at the snippet: yes, line 1313: `ucsi_register_partner(con);` — the ret from `ret = ucsi_register_partner` is not checked… actually, I didn't see \"ret = \" before ucsi_register_partner in the CONNECT_CHANGE branch, so the error is ignored; fine).\n\nOK, now to identify the upstream rework series/commit. From the tree: the driver files under drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/, tcpm/, bus.c, class.c, and the partner name \"%s-partner\". I recall Heikki's series for 6.19 (merged around November 2025): series \"Type-C: make the Type-C ports and their friends into real devices\"? Hmm. Or maybe the series is:\n\ncommit 45f8c1293c7e? I can't fabricate hashes.\n\nLet me look for hints inside the tree: Documentation/driver-api/usb/typec.rst may describe it. Or class.h / bus.c. Let me check bus.c and the documentation.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"The tree is kernel 7.0 (upstream master). Now let me check how ports are registered in this tree (bus.c, `typec_port_register`) and the documentation, to pinpoint the rework."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_0qeiksf6","name":"codebase_memory","arguments":{"tool":"get_code_snippet","arguments":"{\"project\": \"workspace-linux\", \"qualified_name\": \"workspace-linux.drivers.usb.typec.bus.typec_bus\"}"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_bdzgl5wp","name":"codebase_memory","arguments":{"tool":"search_code","arguments":"{\"project\": \"workspace-linux\", \"pattern\": \"int typec_port_register\"}"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"ollama","model":"qwen3.8:27b-q8_0","usage":{"input":38645,"output":6436,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":45081,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787747795632,"responseId":"chatcmpl-404","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},"toolResults":[{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_0qeiksf6","toolName":"codebase_memory","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"name\":\"typec_bus\",\"qualified_name\":\"workspace-linux.drivers.usb.typec.bus.typec_bus\",\"label\":\"Variable\",\"file_path\":\"/workspace/linux/drivers/usb/typec/bus.c\",\"start_line\":564,\"end_line\":571,\"source\":\"const struct bus_type typec_bus = {\\n\\t.name = \\\"typec\\\",\\n\\t.dev_groups = typec_groups,\\n\\t.match = typec_match,\\n\\t.uevent = typec_uevent,\\n\\t.probe = typec_probe,\\n\\t.remove = typec_remove,\\n};\\n\",\"callers\":0,\"callees\":0}\n"}],"details":{},"isError":false,"timestamp":1787748114518},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_bdzgl5wp","toolName":"codebase_memory","content":[{"type":"text","text":"results: 0  (cols: qn label file lines matches in out)\ntotal_grep_matches: 0\ntotal_results: 0\nraw_match_count: 0\nelapsed_ms: 3373\n\n"}],"details":{},"isError":false,"timestamp":1787748114518}]}
